---
layout: base.njk
---
{# One page of one category: every entry filed under any of the subjects it
   gathers, as ordinary blog cards. The plates are for choosing a category;
   once inside one, the entries are what matter.

   The order is the category's own `sort` in category.json — newest first
   unless it asks for "title" — and it is settled in the listings before this
   template sees anything. Nothing here announces which order it is: the cards
   carry dates either way, so an A-Z category reads as an A-Z category only
   from its titles.

   Paging is in category.11tydata.js; membership, URLs and pages are worked out
   in the listings in eleventy_binary/lib/eleventy_config.js.

   The alias is `categoryPage`, never `category`. A page's `category` data is
   merged into its `tags` for every template on the site (see eleventyComputed
   in eleventy_config.js), so an object under that name would come out as a
   subject called "[object Object]" in this page's meta tags. #}
{% import "card.njk" as cards %}
{% set entry = categoryPage.category %}

{# The masthead stands on the category's own picture: .photo-stage with the
   adaptive modifier, which is the site's band-over-a-photograph component —
   washed pale under ink type in light mode, dimmed under paper type in dark,
   with the scrim alphas that were measured for it in theme.css.

   Not a .hero-stage. A hero is a full viewport and would put every entry below
   the fold on a page whose whole job is listing them; it would also be picked
   up by nav_reveal.js, which hides the bar over a leading hero, and a listing
   page should not take the navigation away.

   The picture is the ORIGINAL rather than the _min counterpart: a band is the
   widest surface on the page, past what a thumbnail covers. renderBleed() in
   blocks/render.js takes the same line for the same reason. It is eager, being
   the first thing on the page, and its alt is empty because the title over it
   says what it is.

   With no picture the same band stands on --stage-ground, the scheme's own
   stage gradient, which is what .hero-stage uses when it has no photograph.
   One code path either way. #}
<section class="photo-stage photo-stage-adaptive category-masthead">
  {%- if entry.image %}
  <div class="stage-media">
    <img src="{{ entry.image }}" alt=""{{ entry.image | sizeAttrs | safe }} loading="eager" decoding="async">
  </div>
  {%- endif %}

  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow"><a class="link-underline" href="/categories.html">Categories</a> — {{ entry.count }} {% if entry.count == 1 %}entry{% else %}entries{% endif %}</p>
    <h1 class="display" style="margin-top:1rem;">{{ entry.title }}</h1>
    {%- if entry.description %}
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">{{ entry.description }}</p>
    {%- endif %}

    {#- The subjects this category gathers, each a way into the subject's own
        page. Only the ones something is filed under: a subject with no entries
        has no page to link to. The same row of filing marks a post's header
        carries. -#}
    {%- if entry.subjects.length %}
    <div class="category-masthead-subjects">
      {%- for subject in entry.subjects %}
      <a class="tag-chip" href="{{ subject.href }}">{{ subject.tag }} ({{ subject.count }})</a>
      {%- endfor %}
    </div>
    {%- endif %}
  </div>
</section>

{#- No rule under the band: the band has an edge of its own, and the gradient
    rule is what stands in for one on a page that has none. -#}
<section class="shell" style="padding-block:clamp(2.5rem,6vh,4rem) 2rem;">
  {% if categoryPage.entries.length %}
  <div class="card-grid">
    {% for post in categoryPage.entries %}{{ cards.card(post, settings) }}{% endfor %}
  </div>
  {% else %}
  <p class="lede" style="padding-block:3rem;">Nothing is filed here yet.</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

{% set pager = categoryPage.pager %}
{% include "pagination.njk" %}
